The first steps are lifestyle measures:
- drinking more fluids;
- increasing salt intake, after checking with your doctor that this is safe for you;
- regular exercise training, built up gradually;
- compression garments, such as compression stockings or abdominal binders.

If these don't help enough, medicines can be considered. They work by increasing blood volume, slowing the heart rate or tightening blood vessels. They are best managed together with specialists such as a cardiologist or neurologist.

Findings that support hEDS:
- soft, silky or velvety texture;
- mild semi-transparency;
- subtle hyperextensibility, easily missed if you are expecting the stretch seen in classical EDS;
- easy bruising;
- mildly atrophic scars that are wider than the wound or slightly sunken.

The skin is more fragile than normal, but much less so than in the other EDS types.

These findings should raise concern for another EDS type:
- marked skin transparency, which suggests vascular EDS;
- pronounced hyperextensibility, as expected in classical EDS;
- skin fragility clearly greater than the mild fragility described above.

Acknowledge that some patients do not respond meaningfully to therapy. Patients with intractable, disabling conditions are prone to feeling abandoned and may be vulnerable to potentially toxic therapies or exploitative practitioners. A long-term supportive and protective physician relationship is invaluable here.

Treatment algorithms for fatigue are poorly defined, so set realistic aims:
- improve symptoms;
- maintain function;
- provide social, physical and nutritional support.

Possibly. Ultrasound studies have found changes in the deep fascia of people with hEDS and HSD, for example in the neck, the side of the thigh, the lower back and the hip. The fascia appears 'densified': thicker and stickier, so its layers don't glide over each other as smoothly.

Researchers think this could:
- irritate nerve endings in the fascia and cause pain;
- disturb the signals your body uses to sense joint position, affecting balance and coordination;
- change the way you walk.

This is a promising theory supported by early imaging studies, but it is not yet proven. More research is needed to know how it changes over time and which treatments help.

Sleep hygiene advice:
- avoid caffeine and nicotine near bedtime;
- exercise during the day;
- avoid large late meals;
- keep a regular, relaxing bedtime routine and avoid dwelling on problems at bedtime;
- reserve the bed for sleep (no TV, games or messaging);
- keep the bedroom comfortable, dark, quiet and at a moderate temperature;
- get natural light exposure, especially in the morning.

Introduce changes gradually. Chronic pain may dominate sleep problems and should be managed accordingly.

Commonly associated features not included in the diagnostic criteria:
- chronic fatigue and easy bruising;
- functional bowel disorders: GERD, gastritis, early satiety, delayed gastric emptying, IBS, constipation;
- autonomic dysfunction, Raynaud phenomenon, acrocyanosis;
- reduced efficacy of local anesthetics;
- laryngeal and breathing disorders, including apnea;
- neurologic problems: migraine, Chiari I malformation, CSF leaks, craniocervical instability, tethered cord, entrapment and peripheral neuropathy, small fiber neuropathy;
- mast cell activation disorders and immune deficiency;
- anxiety disorders, autism and ADHD;
- urogynecologic problems: dysmenorrhea, menorrhagia, interstitial cystitis, urinary incontinence, pelvic organ prolapse.

These do not count toward diagnosis, but recognizing them helps connect multisystem complaints and plan care.

Yes. A feeling of incomplete emptying is common in hEDS and HSD, partly because pelvic floor problems are frequent. One example is reduced sensation in the rectum (rectal hyposensitivity), which makes it harder to sense when the bowel is full. Doctors can check how the muscles and nerves involved in emptying work, using:
- anorectal manometry, which measures pressure and sensation;
- a balloon expulsion test;
- defecography, an imaging test of emptying.

Ask your doctor or a gastroenterologist whether these tests are right for you. Knowing the cause helps guide treatment.

Standard treatments apply:
- avoid sudden postural changes;
- consider lower-limb and/or abdominal compression garments;
- exercise to increase muscle strength and tone;
- increase sodium and water intake to expand blood volume (commercial electrolyte tablets can help).

When needed, medications include beta-blockers, midodrine, fludrocortisone, ivabradine or others. These should be started by a clinician experienced in autonomic medicine.

Interviews with 28 families of children aged 5–17 identified these factors.

Adherence was better with:
- parental motivation;
- family routines adapted to include exercise;
- exercise as a family activity;
- the child seeing the benefit.

Adherence was worse with:
- low parental supervision;
- not understanding the treatment;
- not seeing benefit;
- no dedicated time for exercise.

Use this when designing programs: involve parents, explain the rationale, schedule exercise time and make progress visible.

Validating symptoms can be immensely helpful, because many patients have been accused of malingering or given primary psychiatric diagnoses. Prioritize trust, rapport and a supportive relationship.

Anxiety and depression commonly result from the burden of many morbidities. Options include:
- psychological or symptom-oriented counseling, to improve adaptation and acceptance;
- cognitive behavioral therapy, which requires the patient's active participation;
- antidepressants;

This situation is common in hEDS. Neuropathic pain, with allodynia or electric, burning, numb or tingling qualities, often occurs even when anatomical imaging and nerve conduction or EMG studies are normal.

Consider small fiber neuropathy. Skin biopsy may show reduced intraepidermal nerve fiber density, which conventional electrodiagnostics do not detect.

Other contributors to the overall pain picture:
- muscle and tendon spasm;
- trauma from joint instability;
- nerve entrapment;
- secondary osteoarthritis;
- in later stages, central sensitization and generalized hyperalgesia.

Normal standard tests therefore do not exclude a neuropathic mechanism, and treatment should address it.

Build a collaborative relationship with the patient and carers. Family engagement is especially important for children and people with severe fatigue.

Share decisions on:
- excluding other diseases;
- acknowledging the physical, emotional, social, educational and occupational impact;
- setting realistic goals and timelines, with plans for setbacks;
- choosing interventions according to age, severity, preferences and prior responses.

For severe fatigue, coordinate a multidisciplinary team (nursing, OT, dietetics, psychology, physiotherapy, pain management) through a named professional, usually the GP.

Common gynecologic complaints:
- genital mucosal problems, self-reported by 67% of 223 women with EDS;
- heavy menstrual bleeding, in 26–76% of women with hEDS;
- dyspareunia, in 30–57% of women with EDS or hEDS.

Ask about these routinely, because patients may not raise them spontaneously. Heavy bleeding should also prompt assessment of the bleeding tendency and iron status.

Some patients who did not respond to local anesthetics reported that, when they said they could still feel pain, the clinician did not believe them and continued. One recalled feeling sutures being pulled through the skin.

Reduced local anesthetic efficacy is recognized in hEDS. Before procedures:
- ask about past anesthetic failure;
- plan supplemental dosing or alternative analgesia;
- stop and reassess if the patient reports pain.

Disbelief in this situation causes lasting harm and erodes trust.

In this survey, 16% of patients (82 people) had received psychosomatic or similar labels. Such dismissal can:
- delay the correct diagnosis;
- lead to unnecessary antipsychotic prescriptions;
- cause lasting trauma responses to routine clinical encounters.

Functional neurological disorder diagnoses had the highest rejection rate among patients (95%). Before attributing multisystem symptoms to a psychological cause, consider hEDS and related conditions.

For asymptomatic hypermobility, the data are reassuring. In 587 parous women assessed 5–10 years after their first delivery, 46 (7.8%) had hypermobility, defined as a Beighton score of 4 or more. Hypermobility was associated with:
- lower odds of caesarean after full dilation or operative vaginal delivery;
- fewer anal sphincter lacerations;
- no increase in pelvic floor disorders in the first decade after childbirth.

The authors conclude that hypermobility may facilitate spontaneous vaginal birth. Note that the study did not use syndromic criteria, so it does not directly apply to hEDS, where other data suggest more pelvic floor problems.

Gastritis and GERD may need intensive therapy:
- a PPI twice daily before meals;
- a high-dose H2 blocker at bedtime;
- sucralfate;
- over-the-counter antacids.

Investigate and treat H. pylori and SIBO. Endoscopy is indicated for resistant symptoms, but it is often normal. Identify and treat delayed gastric or colonic emptying and causes of anorectal pain and prolapse, ideally with an experienced gastroenterologist.

Treat IBS as usual: diet, fiber, antispasmodics, antidiarrheals and laxatives.

If GI evaluation finds no cause for abdominal pain, consider Doppler ultrasound for vascular compression syndromes.

Most pregnancies in women with hEDS are uncomplicated, but some issues need attention:
- recurrent joint dislocations;
- previous surgical joint stabilization;
- pain, including secondary autonomic pain;
- POTS.

The authors stress that awareness of the condition and a multidisciplinary approach lead to good outcomes for mother and baby. Identify hEDS at booking and coordinate the relevant specialties.

Relaxation techniques may help with pain, sleep problems and comorbid stress or anxiety. Options:
- progressive muscle relaxation (sequentially tensing and relaxing muscle groups);
- visualization with controlled, slow breathing;
- massage, meditation, yoga, and music or art therapy.

In interviews with 17 adults, five themes emerged:
- limitations of healthcare and low professional awareness;
- a restricted life;
- social stigma, including frustration at not keeping up and feelings of shame;
- fear of the unknown, including fear of future decline;
- ways of coping.

Asking about these areas, and pointing patients to reliable information, can help address the psychosocial impact.

The combined prevalence of hEDS/HSD has been estimated at 194 per 100,000, well above the 'rare disease' threshold of 40 per 100,000. Many primary care providers are nevertheless unfamiliar with these conditions, and the time from symptom onset to diagnosis is often 20 years or more.

Consequences of delay:
- iatrogenic harm from unnecessary tests, treatments and surgery;
- worse pain;
- more psychological distress;
- poorer health-related quality of life.

Timely recognition enables early intervention and helps prevent disability.

Insomnia and unrefreshing sleep are common. A study of 115 patients with hEDS found significantly worse sleep quality than in controls.

Poor sleep matters because it worsens fatigue, coordination, cognition and mood. Sleep-maintenance insomnia also impairs endogenous pain inhibition, which amplifies pain.

Contributors to look for:
- pain;
- dysautonomia;
- poor sleep hygiene;
- medications.

Treating sleep problems is a lever for improving both pain and fatigue.

hEDS can coexist with RA, SLE or osteoarthritis. When a rheumatologic disease is present, however, the Feature C complications must be attributable to hypermobility rather than to the inflammatory or degenerative disease.

Feature C requires at least one of:
- daily pain in two or more limbs for at least 3 months;
- chronic widespread pain for at least 3 months;
- atraumatic instability, defined as three or more dislocations of the same joint, two or more dislocations in two different joints at different times, or medically confirmed instability at two or more sites.

In your patient, inflammatory arthritis could fully explain limb pain, so Feature C may not be counted. A diagnosis would then rely on Features A and B (systemic signs and an affected first-degree relative).

Criterion 3 also requires excluding:
- unusual fragility of the skin, eyes, periodontium, vessels or viscera, or a skeletal dysplasia, which suggest another EDS type;
- neuromuscular disorders, other heritable connective tissue disorders and skeletal dysplasias that cause laxity through hypotonia or tissue laxity.

The pathophysiology is poorly understood. Proposed contributors are:
- connective tissue laxity and its functional consequences;
- autonomic dysfunction;
- medication effects;
- comorbid mental health disorders.

Awareness is key to early diagnosis. Management has a limited evidence base, but an integrated multidisciplinary approach is recommended.

The central goals are:
- managing symptoms;
- preventing joint injury;
- educating the patient about the condition.

Based on limited evidence and expert opinion, the mainstays are patient education, physical and occupational therapy, psychological support and self-management.

The course of HSD varies: symptoms may resolve with therapy, persist, or progress to hEDS. hEDS is managed as a lifelong condition because no curative treatment exists. Re-evaluate HSD patients over time.

Activity management, a form of pacing, includes:
- planning each day to balance a variety of activities with rest and sleep;
- spreading demanding tasks across the day or week;
- breaking activities into small, achievable tasks;
- monitoring and regulating activity to avoid boom-and-bust cycles;
- setting goals and prioritizing.

Use this alongside graded exercise and sleep management.

Patients reported hearing:
- 'hypermobility shouldn't be causing pain, it's just the way you are';
- 'you're not hypermobile because you can't touch the floor';
- the condition dismissed as 'the stretchy skin thing'.

Many providers did not know that chronic widespread pain is a common feature, and viewed hypermobility as benign laxity in otherwise healthy people. Few knowledgeable specialists are available for referral.

Counter these misconceptions: chronic widespread pain is a common feature of hEDS/HSD, not a sign that hypermobility is benign.

Fascia is a web of connective tissue that wraps and connects muscles, organs, nerves and other structures throughout the body, in layers just under the skin, around muscles and around organs. It used to be seen as simple 'packaging', but it is now known to be active tissue. It contains nerves and blood vessels and helps:
- transmit force between muscles;
- give your brain information about body position (proprioception);
- regulate pain;
- communicate with cells, the immune system and body fluids.

Because fascia is everywhere in the body, researchers think changes in it could help explain why hEDS affects so many body systems. This is still an emerging area of research.

Patients described these effective coping strategies:
- acceptance;
- building social networks;
- learning about joint hypermobility;
- adapting activities.

Physiotherapists supported regular exercise, and those knowledgeable about JHS/hEDS were described as sources of support and hope.

The authors emphasize that reliable information, for both patients and professionals, reduces misinformation and fear. Clinicians can help by providing trustworthy resources and referring to experienced physiotherapists.
